hi it's michelle from lab muffin beauty  science chemistry phd and skincare nerd  today we're talking about alcohol  not drinking alcohol alcohol in skincare  i knew that  alcohol is one of the most controversial  ingredients in skincare  you'll hear things like alcohol can dry  out your skin give you premature  wrinkles  and kill your skin cells but is this  really true  i've been looking at what the research  says about this topic with stephen  hi everyone i'm stephen from kind of  steven i'm  a cosmetic formulator and a friend of  michelle's and you can find me on  instagram  at kind of stephen today we're going to  be talking about the alcohols in  skincare  why they're there and whether they're  worth avoiding in particular we're going  to be talking about ethyl alcohol or  ethanol or denatured alcohol  since this is the main controversial  alcohol if you like this sort of video  please give it a thumbs up subscribe to  my channel  and click the notification bell let's  start by looking at exactly what alcohol  is  in chemistry an alcohol is any  carbon-based compound that contains an  oxygen bonded to a hydrogen  an oh or hydroxyl group so for example  all of these chemicals are alcohols but  just because they're alcohols doesn't  mean that they have the same properties  on your skin  for example glycerin and glycols are  humectant moisturizers that will hydrate  your skin  sucrose is table sugar settle alcohol is  a fatty alcohol which doesn't evaporate  much at all and it feels oily on your  skin  for some people it's beneficial as a  moisturizer in other people it breaks  them out  so yes some alcohols can be beneficial  for your skin but when people talk about  alcohols in skin care  usually they're referring to the more  volatile alcohols which are the ones  that evaporate quickly off your skin  these have a reputation for being drying  and irritating  and in particular what they mean is  usually ethanol  or ethyl alcohol so what is ethanol  ethanol ethyl alcohol or denatured  alcohol is mostly the same alcohol that  you find in alcoholic drinks  so i think there's some confusion about  what denatured alcohol is  i've read online that it's harsher for  your skin that it's more gentle  that it's another molecule altogether  and that's not the case  so it's still in this case ethanol the  same molecule  except there's been different  ingredients that have been added in to  discourage people from drinking it  why is alcohol in skin care products  ethanol is included in skin care  products for a few different reasons  stephen is a cosmetic formulator who has  lots of experience in using different  ingredients in skin care products  so he is going to take us through some  of these roles so ethanol  is a really useful solvent it can  dissolve things that normally can  dissolve in water but it can also  dissolve  things that normally don't dissolve in  water so a lot of fragrances for example  will come in  a ethanol or alcohol base and that's  because the fragrance oils and perfumes  that don't normally dissolve in water  are able to dissolve in alcohol  salicylic acid for example is an  anti-acne ingredient so it has a very  low solubility in water but  actually dissolves quite readily in  ethanol so a lot of older formulations  with salicylic acid you'll usually see  an ethanol component so ethanol can also  be used as  a solvent to extract ingredients from  plant material for example like vanilla  extract it can also be used to  remove lipids oils and waxes from the  skin  if you look at older toner formulations  and  makeup removers a lot of them also had  ethanol in them to help remove  makeup soil and dirt this is a lot less  common now  but a high concentration of ethanol can  also be used as a preservative in a  cosmetic formula so another property of  ethanol is that it's very volatile which  means that it's very fast evaporating  so that gives it a very quick spreading  texture and also like a very quick  drying  cooling effect on the skin that's often  used in sunscreens to make their texture  lighter and help them evaporate and set  faster ethanol can also be used as a  skin penetration enhancer for certain  ingredients and that means it'll help  get those ingredients deeper into the  skin at a higher concentration than  without it  so a lot of formulas are now replacing  ethanol with  glycols like propane diol or propylene  glycol propane dial and propylene glycol  both  have alcohol functional groups on them  that oh group but they don't have the  volatility  the fast evaporation that ethanol does  so they actually remain on the skin  and they can give like that glowy  glistening effect on the skin  so that's good for some formulations but  not necessarily all formulations  i chatted to susan of mix makeup about  some products she uses that have alcohol  in them and what it's doing in there  i've put the link to her video in the  description so  alcohol is pretty useful for beauty  products but doesn't have a dark side  so i generally try to avoid making  formulations with ethanol if it can be  avoided  that's not necessarily because i think  it's dangerous or harmful just that i  know  that people in the beauty community  really aren't looking for formulations  with ethanol anymore  there are lots of in vitro studies where  the effects of alcohol on cells in a  dish were studied  and the results of these are pretty  scary alcohol can make cells die in a  process called apoptosis  it can make cells produce inflammatory  signals it can denature proteins  and it can stop enzymes from working and  we know that alcohol is a really good  disinfectant  it can kill bacteria very quickly but  skin is very different from naked cells  in a dish  or a single bacterium made up of just  one cell we have about 10 to 20 layers  of dead skin cells on the surface of our  skin  the stratum corneum before we get to the  living layers of cells  and these dead cells aren't just any  dead cells they're toughened with  keratin  and they're sitting in an oily goop  which is really really good at  preventing things from entering and  leaving  so for ethanol to affect our living skin  cells like it does in the in vitro  studies  it would firstly have to get through  this extremely efficient barrier  in a high enough concentration to make a  difference while most of these in vitro  studies used relatively  low concentrations of ethanol somewhere  around 0.5 percent  they also left the cells in contact with  the ethanol for a really long time  sometimes 24 hours in a container where  the alcohol couldn't evaporate  but ethanol when we apply it to our skin  is very volatile  it evaporates very easily and you'll  have felt this when you feel the cool  sensation from an alcohol containing  product  this is the alcohol evaporating and  taking some of your body heat with it it  turns out that when you apply ethanol on  your skin  the vast majority of it will evaporate  so one experiment used pig skin to take  a look at how quickly 100  ethanol evaporated off the skin and what  they found was that after 10 seconds  more than 50 of it had already  evaporated overall they found that  about three percent less than three  percent was left on the skin  now there's some issues with that three  percent uh because they think that some  of the alcohol had actually leaked under  the chamber  of their test equipment so it's actually  likely that it's much much less  so what we can infer from this is that  if you're using a skincare product with  ethanol in the formula  maybe around five to ten percent then  more than 97 of that is going to  evaporate off the skin  very quickly so all of these studies  showing that alcohol was bad for cells  in a dish  we can't immediately apply them to the  way that we use alcohol in skin care the  most relevant studies for us would be  the ones where they looked at alcohol  being applied to human skin  and observing what happened as i've  mentioned before it's often really hard  to find good skin care studies because  most scientific research is funded by  the government so there needs to be a  really good public health reason for  doing the research  otherwise it might be funded by a beauty  brand or an ingredient company  in which case there needs to be a strong  commercial reason for doing the study  luckily there is a relevant situation  where people do end up applying lots and  lots of alcohol to their skin  in much larger quantities and much more  often than we would in skincare that's  in healthcare where alcohol-based hand  sanitizers are used by healthcare  workers to prevent spreading of diseases  in particular nurses have to disinfect  their hands between patients  and this adds up one study found that on  average  nurses had to clean their hands 55 times  a day lots of nurses end up with crack  skin irritation and blisters on their  hands from this repeated cleaning  it's been found that skin irritation  makes people less likely to follow hand  hygiene procedures  and so there's a really compelling  public health reason to research this  the main methods for disinfecting hands  are washing your hands with soap and  water  and alcohol-based hand rubs so there's  been quite a few clinical studies  comparing soap and water versus alcohol  hand rubs  and between different hand rub formulas  on things like skin irritation  dryness cracking and so on so what do  these studies tell us  what a lot of these studies are showing  us is that even using  a very large amount of ethanol in the  skin doesn't really have a huge  long-term impact let's look at one of  the more recent detailed studies on  alcohol-based hand rubs to get an idea  of how these studies are conducted this  article which i've linked in the  description was published in 2017  in the international journal of cosmetic  science it's open access so you can  download it and read it as well the  clinical part of this study involved  testing three different alcohols at 70  concentration  on the inner forearms of 25 white female  volunteers  aged 35 to 50. a fourth area of skin had  water applied as a comparison the  solutions were rubbed into skin for  about 10 seconds  either 20 times a day or 100 times a day  the skin was also washed six times a day  with hand wash and water to mimic what  would happen for a typical nurse  this process was done for 14 days around  every two days the scientists measured  skin hydration  redness and trans epidermal water loss  this is how much water was evaporating  through the skin a way of measuring skin  barrier function  so these hand wrap studies usually use  alcohol concentrations far higher than  we would see in skincare plus they used  it many more times a day plus there's  that extra irritation from the repeated  cleansing as well it's likely that  whatever effects these studies find will  be a bigger version of what would happen  to us  when we apply a moisturizer or sunscreen  with 5 or 10 ethanol in them  so what were the findings of these  studies one of the biggest questions is  does alcohol cause  inflammation inflammation isn't good for  skin it's been linked to lots of skin  issues like acne rosacea and premature  aging  and in some in vitro cell studies  alcohol does seem to be able to cause  cells to release inflammatory markers  but will this happen on actual skin in  these hand rub studies  inflammation was measured by looking at  skin redness inflammation causes blood  flow to increase  and so you get a reddening effect and in  several of these studies  scientists found that ethanol didn't  seem to be linked to skin redness  compared to no ethanol this was even the  case in a study where 70 ethanol was  applied to the skin  a hundred times a day for 14 days there  was no statistically significant  difference in redness compared to water  applied 100 times a day  so that is a total of 1400 applications  of ethanol over two weeks  another question about alcohol is  whether or not it can disrupt your skin  barrier to a harmful extent  your skin's main job is to act as a  barrier between you  and the outside world the top dead  layers of skin the stratum corneum  are essential to this function to act as  an effective barrier this stratum  corneum has to keep itself maintained  the dead skin cells have to continuously  shed  smoothly and the oily lipids in between  the cells have to stay in an organized  formation  on isolated skin samples in vitro  enzymes that are important in cell  shedding and in the production of oily  lipids can be disturbed  and so they don't work as well there are  also some in vitro studies that suggest  that alcohol  can temporarily modify how molecules are  arranged in your skin or it can remove  lipids from your skin  in a process called lipid extraction  this isn't like it's making your skin  fall apart or anything  it's much more subtle microscopic  changes for example  how the oily molecules are arranged  relative to each other  and this effect doesn't last for long  it's thought that this is how  ethanol helps ingredients penetrate your  skin but these findings aren't really  consistent  many times skin samples even if they  were left with alcohol for a really long  time  there weren't big negative effects and  many of these studies do actually point  out that the conditions that they use  don't reflect how we would use  alcohol on our skin in the clinical  studies ethanol doesn't really seem to  disrupt the barrier much  skin barrier disruption is usually  measured through trans-epidermal water  loss this is how much water is escaping  through skin  into the air and so a more disruptive  barrier would cause  greater water loss in multiple hand rub  studies changes in trans-epidermal water  loss were minor  even with 20 applications of alcohol a  day there was a measurable difference in  one study but that was when ethanol was  applied a hundred times a day  much more than we would in skincare  unless you have a very strange skincare  routine  one thing that can seem to happen with  alcohol is dehydration there have been  mixed results in clinical studies  some have found that alcohol does  decrease hydration while others haven't  for example in the kartner study that i  mentioned earlier skin hydration wasn't  significantly different between ethanol  and water whether you applied it 20  times a day or 100 times a day  in another study skin oil content didn't  change significantly  after applying hand rails containing 60  to 80  alcohol 50 times a day for a week  however there are ways to help with  dehydration and dryness  one study looked at adding moisturizing  ingredients like glycerin and lanolin  and fatty alcohols to hand rubs this  study used hand rubs containing propanol  which has been found in other studies to  be more irritating than ethanol they  found that these moisturizing  ingredients decreased the amount of  redness and dryness that the propanol  caused  in skin care propanol is really rare and  we almost always find ethanol combined  with these moisturizing ingredients  what about stinging one reason that we  tend to be a bit scared of alcohol is  that you sometimes get that painful  stinging sensation  but multiple studies have found that  that stinging painful sensation  doesn't actually mean that harm is being  done to your skin  alcohol does tend to sting your skin if  it's already damaged  the alcohol can go through the skin and  touch the nerve endings  which caused the pain this is like a  more sensitive version of what happens  when water gets into a paper cut  so what does this mean for alcohol in  skin care these studies on alcohol hand  rubs aren't done under the same  conditions as we would use  alcohol in skincare but they're a lot  closer than in vitro studies the studies  were mostly on  arm skin which is thicker and less  sensitive than face skin plus these  studies only lasted a week or two but  they use  far far higher concentrations of alcohol  and many many more times than we would  use  alcohol in a skincare routine some of  these studies also introduce  extra irritation from cleansing many  more times than we would in a skincare  routine  there's also the fact that in skin care  products we have other ingredients like  glycerin which will reduce the impact of  alcohol  in terms of dryness or redness or  irritation so overall the evidence seems  to point towards alcohol not being a big  deal in skincare  given how small the impact was under the  much more extreme conditions in these  hand rub studies  i hope you enjoyed this video if you did  click the thumbs up subscribe to my  channel and click the notification bell  and if you have any of the topics that  you would like us to cover  leave a comment below with your  suggestion you can also follow us on  instagram and check out our blogs  and drop a like if you like this video  and subscribe  if you loved it thanks stay sciency

